WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.410
The shortest route between Russia and the
United States is over the north pole.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:09.690
So if the Cold War had ever become Hot, the bombs
would have been going straight over here.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:13.530
This is Tuktoyaktuk, on the Arctic Ocean,
in the Canadian Northwest Territories.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.740
And that behind me with the domes

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:19.920
was once the military's DEW Line:
the Distant Early Warning Line.

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.220
Or at least, part of it.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:23.080
The DEW Line was built in the 1950s:

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.530
57 radar bases ranging all the way
from the Aleutian Islands to Greenland.

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:29.500
They looked to the sky around the clock,

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.540
and if any aircraft showed up on radar
that weren't supposed to be there:

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:36.820
well, there was a chance that those were
Soviet bombers carrying nuclear weapons

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:38.760
to North American targets,

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.899
and the governments would have somewhere
between an hour and four hours

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.180
to scramble some sort of response.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.560
Construction of the DEW Line was
an enormous operation.

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.860
Thousands and thousands of people
had to be transported to the Arctic,

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:55.480
provided with food and housing and
the hundreds of thousands of tons of

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:58.440
equipment and supplies that they'd need
to build the Line.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:00.080
But the military is good at logistics,

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:03.579
or at least, the vast number of
civilian contractors they hired were.

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:07.020
All the bases were completed in
just two and a half years,

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.940
and North America was prepared
for Soviet attack,

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:14.060
at the cost of around half a billion
dollars from the two governments.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:16.240
There were other costs too, less financial:

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.660
for a lot of the folks that lived
near the DEW Line sites,

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:22.820
the sudden influx of people and equipment
destroyed their way of life;

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:26.220
although that's a story that
I am not qualified to tell.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:29.760
The construction also contaminated
a lot of the local environment:

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.840
standards then weren't
what they are now,

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.320
and the cleanup is still going on.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:38.140
And for all that, the DEW Line lasted about
five years until it was obsolete.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.840
Because shortly after it was completed, both
the Soviet Union and the United States

00:01:41.840 --> 00:01:46.690
started to put their nuclear warheads not on aircraft,
but on intercontinental ballistic missiles.

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:50.359
They didn't eight or nine hours
of flight over the pole, instead

00:01:50.359 --> 00:01:54.440
the first wave of attack would come from rockets
that would take half an hour

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:57.109
and go literally over the top
of the DEW Line.

00:01:57.109 --> 00:02:00.460
Half of these stations were shut down 
a few years later.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.420
The rest of the Line,
including this station here,

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.649
well, they played a role in air defense.

00:02:05.649 --> 00:02:10.780
If they weren't here, then the Soviet Union could have
theoretically still sent bombers over the pole.

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:14.450
But they weren't the shield that
North America hoped that they would be.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:17.020
US President Eisenhower, in his memoirs,

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:20.890
said that military technology... well,
"if it works, it's obsolete".

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.240
The DEW Line kept running, though,
at least until the 90s,

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.160
when a more modern system was constructed.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.675
Now, this site is part of the
North Warning System,

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.380
part of a modern radar defence around
the whole continent

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:34.060
that can detect basically
anything inbound at any altitude.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.900
Which is why I'm not allowed near it.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.600
Governments on this side of the pole want
to be sure: the Cold War might be over,

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.580
but just in case,
they'd better keep watching.

